1
00:00:02,196 --> 00:00:04,809
The problem with algorithms is that, well,

2
00:00:04,921 --> 00:00:08,431
complex algorithms are complex. Okay, that's

3
00:00:08,431 --> 00:00:10,050
a silly sentence. What do I mean with that?

4
00:00:10,756 --> 00:00:13,641
I mean that coming up with the best possible

5
00:00:13,801 --> 00:00:15,356
algorithm for a given problem

6
00:00:15,901 --> 00:00:18,643
can be very hard. For tricky problems, it's

7
00:00:18,643 --> 00:00:20,069
of course the hardest.

8
00:00:21,015 --> 00:00:23,836
And that's the whole idea behind being asked

9
00:00:23,836 --> 00:00:26,577
for algorithms in interview situations.

10
00:00:27,026 --> 00:00:30,232
Interviewers want to see how you can come up

11
00:00:30,248 --> 00:00:32,893
with algorithms, how good you are when it

12
00:00:32,893 --> 00:00:36,210
comes to solving problems. So that's why

13
00:00:36,482 --> 00:00:39,110
algorithms are so popular in interviews,

14
00:00:39,223 --> 00:00:41,546
because coming up with the right, with the

15
00:00:41,546 --> 00:00:43,726
best possible algorithm is hard.

16
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:45,392
But here's the good news.

17
00:00:46,017 --> 00:00:48,581
It's not the best possible solution that

18
00:00:48,581 --> 00:00:51,497
counts in interviews, and also not as a

19
00:00:51,561 --> 00:00:52,699
developer. You

20
00:00:53,228 --> 00:00:56,657
rarely will find the best possible solution

21
00:00:57,154 --> 00:00:59,720
right at the start. Rarely the first

22
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:01,886
solution you come up with is the best

23
00:01:01,902 --> 00:01:02,576
possible one.

24
00:01:03,106 --> 00:01:05,801
Instead, more often than not, you will come

25
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:09,123
up with a solution and then you'll be able

26
00:01:09,123 --> 00:01:11,787
to gradually improve it with colleagues,

27
00:01:11,931 --> 00:01:14,819
with help of the interviewer, or simply over

28
00:01:14,884 --> 00:01:18,045
time as you get better and find new patterns

29
00:01:18,189 --> 00:01:20,564
and approaches. And just to make this clear

30
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:23,853
right away, also in interviews, no one is

31
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:26,468
expecting you to always come up with just

32
00:01:26,596 --> 00:01:28,343
the best solution and nothing else.

33
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,060
Instead, interviewers also just want to see

34
00:01:32,445 --> 00:01:35,249
how you approach a problem. They want to see

35
00:01:35,249 --> 00:01:37,780
how you think. And even if you then come up

36
00:01:37,893 --> 00:01:40,585
with, let's say, a wrong solution, if the

37
00:01:40,633 --> 00:01:43,405
way you think makes sense, that's perfectly

38
00:01:43,437 --> 00:01:43,725
fine.

39
00:01:44,959 --> 00:01:47,490
So how can we solve problems then? How can

40
00:01:47,523 --> 00:01:50,022
we come up with algorithms? Is there a kind

41
00:01:50,022 --> 00:01:52,906
of a blueprint we can apply to any problem

42
00:01:52,954 --> 00:01:53,579
we're facing?

43
00:01:54,477 --> 00:01:57,361
Well, kind of. There isn't a blueprint in

44
00:01:57,361 --> 00:02:00,790
the sense of follow these 4 steps and you'll

45
00:02:00,806 --> 00:02:02,120
have the best solution,

46
00:02:02,665 --> 00:02:05,117
but there is a certain way of approaching

47
00:02:05,229 --> 00:02:07,873
problems that will maximize your chances of

48
00:02:07,873 --> 00:02:10,885
finding some solution which you eventually

49
00:02:10,917 --> 00:02:11,686
can then improve.

50
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:15,244
And it all starts with a very simple step.

51
00:02:15,869 --> 00:02:17,872
The first step really just is that you

52
00:02:18,048 --> 00:02:20,419
verify the problem and the problem inputs.

53
00:02:20,869 --> 00:02:24,209
Seriously, it's super easy to come up with a

54
00:02:24,306 --> 00:02:25,398
bad solution,

55
00:02:25,992 --> 00:02:28,754
be that in an interview or in a task you

56
00:02:28,866 --> 00:02:32,464
have to solve on your job, simply because

57
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,097
you don't get the problem correctly or

58
00:02:35,370 --> 00:02:38,775
because you assume a certain kind of input,

59
00:02:38,984 --> 00:02:41,409
let's say parameters in a function, that

60
00:02:41,409 --> 00:02:42,790
just isn't there.

61
00:02:43,593 --> 00:02:45,231
So therefore, it's super important that you

62
00:02:45,343 --> 00:02:47,479
verify the problem and the problem inputs.

63
00:02:47,913 --> 00:02:50,594
For example, that you verify whether, let's

64
00:02:50,594 --> 00:02:52,729
say, some array of data is of a fixed

65
00:02:52,729 --> 00:02:56,165
length, if the order matters of the results

66
00:02:56,165 --> 00:02:58,349
your function generates or of the inputs

67
00:02:58,349 --> 00:02:58,879
you're getting.

68
00:02:59,537 --> 00:03:02,025
Things like that need to be verified because

69
00:03:02,025 --> 00:03:04,755
it heavily influences what your algorithm

70
00:03:04,755 --> 00:03:05,381
needs to do.

71
00:03:06,762 --> 00:03:09,475
And then as a next step, you should of

72
00:03:09,507 --> 00:03:11,675
course think about the problem. Now that you

73
00:03:11,675 --> 00:03:13,569
clarified it, it's time to think about it.

74
00:03:14,003 --> 00:03:16,411
And especially in an interview situation,

75
00:03:16,572 --> 00:03:18,559
but also when working on a problem with

76
00:03:18,575 --> 00:03:20,916
colleagues, you should definitely do this

77
00:03:21,365 --> 00:03:23,753
loudly. Think about it loudly.

78
00:03:24,426 --> 00:03:27,472
Derive a verbal solution. Don't start

79
00:03:27,472 --> 00:03:28,514
writing code immediately.

80
00:03:29,059 --> 00:03:31,448
Here in this course, we did that by going

81
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:33,483
through some slides, which I of course

82
00:03:33,483 --> 00:03:36,064
prepared in advance. In this module, we'll

83
00:03:36,112 --> 00:03:39,110
do it together with less slides on concrete

84
00:03:39,238 --> 00:03:41,979
problems. And if you're in an interview and

85
00:03:41,979 --> 00:03:43,807
you don't know in advance which problem

86
00:03:43,807 --> 00:03:45,907
you're going to face, of course you can't

87
00:03:45,907 --> 00:03:48,520
prepare slides. Then you should simply talk

88
00:03:48,632 --> 00:03:50,939
about the problem and your thoughts about it

89
00:03:51,035 --> 00:03:53,311
and how you plan on solving it with your

90
00:03:53,439 --> 00:03:54,336
interview partner.

91
00:03:55,105 --> 00:03:57,156
And this doesn't need to be the final or

92
00:03:57,188 --> 00:03:59,945
best possible solution. You will derive a

93
00:03:59,945 --> 00:04:02,380
solution the more you talk about it. And

94
00:04:02,653 --> 00:04:04,656
that's, by the way, probably an experience

95
00:04:04,656 --> 00:04:05,969
you've made in your life before.

96
00:04:06,562 --> 00:04:09,222
When you're facing a problem, just talking

97
00:04:09,222 --> 00:04:11,802
about it often makes it easier to come up

98
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:13,773
with a solution for that problem. And

99
00:04:13,869 --> 00:04:15,279
programming is no exception.

100
00:04:16,258 --> 00:04:18,426
Now, thinking about a problem and coming up

101
00:04:18,426 --> 00:04:20,417
with different solution strategies,

102
00:04:20,995 --> 00:04:23,468
that's also where experience and practicing

103
00:04:23,564 --> 00:04:25,587
helps though. Of course, you can come up

104
00:04:25,587 --> 00:04:28,188
with a solution to a problem without, well,

105
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:28,766
having

106
00:04:29,345 --> 00:04:30,372
ever written any code,

107
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:33,648
but chances are that your solution might be

108
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,377
unrealistic, might not follow common

109
00:04:36,425 --> 00:04:37,999
patterns or practices,

110
00:04:38,577 --> 00:04:40,520
or you won't be able to implement it in

111
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:40,809
code.

112
00:04:41,402 --> 00:04:43,566
So therefore, of course, you need to

113
00:04:43,566 --> 00:04:46,259
practice. You need to solve tons of

114
00:04:46,291 --> 00:04:48,808
problems, come up with algorithms, explore

115
00:04:49,016 --> 00:04:51,854
common algorithms, and get a feeling for

116
00:04:52,062 --> 00:04:54,210
common approaches. For example, in this

117
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:56,150
course, you often saw that we can use

118
00:04:56,150 --> 00:04:59,163
recursion to split a problem into smaller

119
00:04:59,356 --> 00:04:59,805
problems.

120
00:05:00,382 --> 00:05:02,209
And if you have that in mind, that is

121
00:05:02,385 --> 00:05:04,277
automatically something which you can apply

122
00:05:04,549 --> 00:05:06,794
on any problem you're facing. You can

123
00:05:06,794 --> 00:05:09,325
consider if that might make sense there. So

124
00:05:09,374 --> 00:05:11,296
that is definitely an important aspect.

125
00:05:12,241 --> 00:05:14,132
Well, once you then have your

126
00:05:15,045 --> 00:05:17,320
plan on how you might wanna tackle that

127
00:05:17,384 --> 00:05:17,752
problem,

128
00:05:18,281 --> 00:05:20,268
of course it's time to write down a first

129
00:05:20,268 --> 00:05:22,527
version in code. And that could also be

130
00:05:22,527 --> 00:05:23,392
pseudocode.

131
00:05:23,953 --> 00:05:26,660
Of course, probably not on your real job,

132
00:05:26,997 --> 00:05:28,551
but especially in an interview,

133
00:05:29,191 --> 00:05:31,114
sometimes you don't even need to write code

134
00:05:31,306 --> 00:05:34,158
in, let's say, JavaScript or Python. It's

135
00:05:34,206 --> 00:05:35,696
instead fine if you come up with some

136
00:05:35,744 --> 00:05:38,660
pseudocode which you won't actually run, but

137
00:05:38,676 --> 00:05:40,811
which clearly describes what you plan on

138
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:44,230
doing. Of course, you can also just write a

139
00:05:44,632 --> 00:05:47,408
first version, a first draft in a real, in

140
00:05:47,425 --> 00:05:49,046
an actual programming language.

141
00:05:50,009 --> 00:05:52,449
Well, once you got that first version down,

142
00:05:52,577 --> 00:05:55,964
once you got some solution that works, of

143
00:05:56,028 --> 00:05:58,083
course you should verify whether it catches

144
00:05:58,179 --> 00:06:00,186
all possible cases you can come up with.

145
00:06:00,828 --> 00:06:02,738
And then of course you should also derive

146
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:06,109
its time complexity to judge how well this

147
00:06:06,799 --> 00:06:09,835
solution does. Now, just because you maybe

148
00:06:09,835 --> 00:06:12,308
have a bad time complexity, let's say

149
00:06:12,308 --> 00:06:14,573
quadratic time, does not mean that it's a

150
00:06:14,573 --> 00:06:17,223
bad solution. For some problems, you just

151
00:06:17,223 --> 00:06:19,311
can't do better. Think about the

152
00:06:19,311 --> 00:06:21,736
permutations example from the last module.

153
00:06:22,122 --> 00:06:24,499
There we had factorial time and we weren't

154
00:06:24,515 --> 00:06:26,298
able to improve that. But you should at

155
00:06:26,298 --> 00:06:28,852
least consider improving it and have a look

156
00:06:28,932 --> 00:06:30,972
at your algorithm and see if there is maybe

157
00:06:30,972 --> 00:06:32,193
something you can do

158
00:06:32,722 --> 00:06:35,657
Like for example, store intermediate values

159
00:06:35,705 --> 00:06:37,999
or anything like that that could speed up

160
00:06:37,999 --> 00:06:40,308
your algorithm and that could ultimately

161
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:42,201
lead to a better time complexity.

162
00:06:42,970 --> 00:06:45,504
And then, well, you go back to writing down

163
00:06:45,552 --> 00:06:47,701
a first version of the improved algorithm

164
00:06:48,263 --> 00:06:50,011
and you see how that does.

165
00:06:50,765 --> 00:06:53,555
Now that's a rough blueprint, but of course

166
00:06:53,555 --> 00:06:56,667
it all relies on practicing and experience

167
00:06:56,779 --> 00:06:58,607
and this course helps you with it and

168
00:06:58,623 --> 00:07:00,628
towards the end of the course, I will also

169
00:07:00,692 --> 00:07:03,200
give you more resources that will also help

170
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,085
you with practicing.
